    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0153" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/771: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Castle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-03-22T11:00:00+09:00">March 22, 1930—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 22—4:31 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>54. Your telegram No. 53, March 20, 5 p.m. I think that there is no danger that
                    this matter will arise unless it is mentioned in the press. The Government here
                    has not considered it, so Shidehara tells me; one reason is that it would
                    scarcely be possible to arrive at a formula by which China would not be
                    irritated. We are not more sensitive than he is on this point. He was told that,
                    despite anything the press might say, we should always consider the Kellogg Pact
                    as effective as in any other connection so far as China was concerned and that
                    there was no necessity of committing this to paper. Please repeat to
                        London.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d70-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Castle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d70-f1" n="91" xml:id="d70-f1-content">
                <p>Transmitted to the American delegation as Department’s telegram No. 242, March
                    22, 10 a.m.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
